# ── Secrets & credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore

# Certificates (public certs are usually fine, but excluding to be safe)
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.ca-bundle

# SSH
*.pub
id_rsa
id_ed25519
id_ecdsa
id_dsa
known_hosts

# GPG
*.gpg
*.asc
secring.*

# Generic secret files. These target secret DATA — a file whose CONTENT is a
# credential. They are substring rules, so they also match any source file with
# one of these words in its NAME, which is a different thing entirely.
*secret*
*secrets*
*password*
*passwords*
*credentials*
*token*
*tokens*
*.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample

# ── Role CODE is not secret DATA ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A file under a role's tasks/, handlers/ or templates/ is Ansible logic. It
# READS a credential at runtime (declared var first, Vault fallback, no_log) and
# CONTAINS none, so the substring rules above must not reach it.
#
# This is deliberately a structural rule, not another per-file exception. It
# replaces nine hand-maintained negations that had to be appended one at a time,
# each after the same failure: the file exists on the author's workstation and
# nowhere else — not in the repo, not in a clone, not in CI. `import_tasks` then
# fails at PARSE time, so the role is not degraded, it is unrunnable. Local lint
# passes because the file is right there; only a clean `git clone` reproduces it.
# It cost this repo `vault_pki` (shipped with no tasks/main.yml) and `semaphore`
# (shipped unrunnable). Add a file, not a rule.
#
# NOTE for anyone porting a role in: upstream defends against this by NAMING the
# file to dodge the rule (`resolve_secrets.yml`, `resolve_ansible_secrets.yml`),
# usually with a header comment saying so. That comment is only true for the rule
# in the repo it was written for — upstream's is `**/secrets.yml`, this repo's is
# `*secrets*`, and the same filename escapes one and not the other. Re-test; do
# not trust the comment.
#
# files/ and vars/ are deliberately NOT included: files/ demonstrably holds real
# captured state (see the artifactory state rules below) and vars/ can hold
# values rather than references. Those stay under the blanket.
!ansible/roles/*/tasks/**
!ansible/roles/*/handlers/**
!ansible/roles/*/templates/**
# Same rule for task files that live outside a role (playbook-level tasks/ dirs).
!**/tasks/*.yml

# Vault / encrypted files
*.vault
vault_pass*
vault-pass*
.vault_pass*
ansible-vault-password*

# API keys and config with embedded secrets
*_rsa
*_dsa
*_ecdsa
*_ed25519
kubeconfig
*kubeconfig*
*.kubeconfig

